  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nQuiet, dry and warm conditions continue once again today. We \nare up to 11 days with no precipitation at the office.\n\nA weak cold front is currently dropping through MI and is \nexpected to continue a slow eastward advance through the region \novernight. A very dry pre-frontal environment and limited \nmoisture along the front itself limits rain chances with REFS \nprobabilities of measurable precipitation not rising above 40% \nuntil you get north of I-80. Between I-80 and US-422, 10-40% \nprobabilities indicate the chance for a sprinkle or two but it \nis expected that the dry streak at the office will continue. \n\nModels have also taken a step back on anticipated rain this \nweekend, depicting a weaker overall wave struggling to \nappreciably move eastward under a building rex block. As such, \nany rain for the region with that wave may be pushed into \nSunday night or Monday, if occuring at all. If our dry streak \ncontinues through midnight Monday morning, we would reach 15 \ndays without measurable precipitation at the office and move \ninto the top 10 for longest such streaks ever. Only two such \nstreaks have eclipsed 12 days this century (both ending at 13 \ndays). \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nLonger range ensembles continue to tease a possible pattern \nchange on the horizon as a closed 500-mb low develops across \nthe central CONUS and progresses eastward. However, much is \nstill needed in the way of agreement on timing and progression \nbefore we can meaningfully talk about it. Clustered ensembles \nare in good agreement out to Monday, showing a large open wave \ntrough sat across the Rockies. By Tuesday, solutions diverge on \ndepth and speed of the wave as ~70% close off the low while ~30%\nkeep it an open wave. This makes speculation about the long- \nrange forecast difficult but likely hints at our warm, dry and \ncalm weather continuing into at least early to perhaps the \nmiddle of next week. If consensus on the track and movement of \nthis low increases then we can begin to talk about a possible \npattern change over to cooler, wetter and breezier conditions.",
